Project Title:
Student Engagement with Interactive eBooks and Its Impact on Academic Success

Project Abstract:
Analyze log file data from an intermediate programming course in Python to determine the percentage of people who do the different types of activities and look for correlations between usage types and midterm scores. Students will have access to anonymous log file data for that course, which uses a free and interactive ebook for pre readings, lecture practice, Peer Instruction, and midterms.


Full Description:
I have been using the open-source ebook platform Runestone in SI 206 (now 201) since I started teaching at Michigan in 2018. I research new types of practice problems such as Parsons puzzles in which students put mixed-up blocks in order to solve a problem. I also do active learning in lectures including Process Oriented Guided Inquiry Learning (POGIL), Peer Instruction, and a mix of solving Parsons puzzles and writing code with Parsons puzzles as help for struggling students. POGIL is a structured inquiry process where students work in groups to study a model, in this case code, and answer questions about it. In Peer Instruction, as described by Eric Mazur, the instructor shows a difficult multiple choice question, students answer individually (vote 1), then discuss with nearby peers, and then answer individually again (vote 2). Next the instructor shows a histogram of the two votes and leads a discussion about the question. Midterms have been conducted in Runestone since 2020. They include multiple-choice questions, fill in the blank questions, Parsons puzzles, and write code questions.


We have anonymous log file data from the ebook and I want to analyze it. In particular I want to determine the percentage of students who do each type of activity in the ebook, how the percentage changes over the chapter and semester, and look for correlations with usage patterns and midterm scores.


Project Outcome:  
CSV files with data for each activity in each chapter and average data for each type of activity. 
Final report/summary style presentation to summarize student findings.
Final report for students to summarize their findings and analysis.
If students are interested, they can also look for positive correlations between scores on practice activities such as Peer Instruction and midterms.  

